;; fib.core.alias (owner L12): Clojure's spellings of builtins that fibber has under another name
;; (stdlib §3 N12, §4.1 rows bit-shift-left bit-shift-right unsigned-bit-shift-right == not=).
;; It replaces nothing: shl, sar, shr, = and != keep their names, and the cases and the compiler that
;; use them are untouched. Each alias is one generic function over the builtin protocol of the
;; method it names, so it is monomorphised to the builtin's instruction and takes the builtin's
;; typing: both operands one type, no promotion.
;;
;; Decided here, where the page is silent or a stage limit decides:
;;  * The shift distance has the type of the value, as the builtin's has (`shl (self n: Self)`), and
;;    is taken modulo the width, as Java's is (types §2.12): (bit-shift-left 1 64) is 1 and
;;    (bit-shift-left 1 -1) is the minimum. Clojure's distance is a long; a literal that adopts the
;;    width of its operand is L26 (a), so until then the distance for an i32 is written 3i32.
;;  * bit-shift-right is the arithmetic shift (sar) and unsigned-bit-shift-right the logical one
;;    (shr), each at the width of the operand: (unsigned-bit-shift-right -1i8 1i8) is 127i8. Java's
;;    >>> promotes a byte to int first; fibber promotes nothing.
;;  * == is = and not= is != on one type (the rows say "alias"), so (== 0.0 -0.0) is true and
;;    (== nan nan) false, as IEEE and Clojure's are. Across numeric types (a literal by L19, a
;;    variable by L26 b) and with more than two arguments they are not offered: no Rust macro of
;;    E1 folds them (R6a's list has + - * < > <= >= = and the bit operators), so (== a b c) is
;;    reported to the lead, not written here.
(ns fib.core.alias)

(defun bit-shift-left (a: t n: t) :where ((Bits t)) -> t (shl a n))
(defun bit-shift-right (a: t n: t) :where ((Bits t)) -> t (sar a n))
(defun unsigned-bit-shift-right (a: t n: t) :where ((Bits t)) -> t (shr a n))

(defun == (a: t b: t) :where ((Eq t)) -> bool (= a b))
(defun not= (a: t b: t) :where ((Eq t)) -> bool (!= a b))
